ing fixation and stabilization of these catalytically active entities within the channels (Figure $4\mathrm{aright,b,c},$ ). Interestingly, the simplicity of the synthetic soft procedure used, consisting basically of suspending the preformed MOF in $\mathrm{{Ru}(\mathrm{{III})}}$ or $\mathrm{Fe(III)}$ aqueous solutions, allows the gram-scale preparation of these host−guest compounds.
Aiming at confirming the enhanced catalytic activity and reusability of these supported complexes, different experiments were carried out. Ruthenium compounds are known to perform well in different alcohol dehydrogenation reactions.44 However, their implementation is still weak in dehydrogenating imination reactions,45 as the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ produced during the reaction tends to further reduce the imine to amine. In fact, among the wide variety of Ru compounds that were tested in solution, only $[\mathrm{Ru(\dot{H}_{2}O)}_{6}]^{3+}$ in $\mathrm{HBF}_{4}$ aqueous solution showed catalytic activity for imine formation, most-likely due to its acidity, accessibility, and water-stability. However, such a hexa-aquocomplex in the homogeneous phase suffered a rapid degradation under reaction conditions. In order to overcome such limitation, $\mathrm{\big[Ru\big(H_{2}O\big)_{6}\big]^{3+}}$ was first supported in alumina, silica, titania, and magnesia, but no real catalytic and stability improvement took place. The degradation was only attenuated by encapsulating the $\left[\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{\check{O}})_{6}\right]^{3+}$ complex in anionic faujasite-type zeolites and much improved when supported in the $\mathrm{Ni^{II}}_{2}\mathrm{\hat{\{Ni^{II}}}_{4}\lbrack C u^{I I}}_{2}(M e_{3}m p b a)_{2}\rbrack_{3}\mathrm{\hat{\cdot}\hat{\ s}4H_{2}O}$ MOF (Figure 4a). Indeed, the resulting $[\mathbf{Ru(H_{2}O)_{6}}]^{3+}\mathbf{-MOF}$ catalyst gave high yields $(\sim90\%)$ for the imination of 4-vinylbenzyl alcohol and 3,4-dimethylaniline, which is the highest among all Ru catalysts tested. No leaching processes and/or degradation of the catalyst were observed in toluene as solvent and, consequently, much better recyclability (at least 5 cycles without loss of catalytic activity) was observed. Here, it must be underlined that the key role played by SC-XRD is to unveil both the crystal structures of the host−guest material and also to follow the cation exchange process (Figure 4a).
Dealing with $[\mathbf{\bar{Fe}(H_{2}O)_{6}}]^{3+}\mathbf{-\bar{MOF}}$ , the resolution of its crystal structure proved invaluable not just to characterize the material but also to choose the most appropriate catalytic reaction. Indeed, the resulting stabilized $\mathrm{\hat{Fe}^{I I I}}\mathrm{-}0$ sites were capable of efficiently and chemoselectively catalyzing the acetylene hydrogenation in ethylene flows under simulating front-end industrial conditions46 (Figure 5). These results confirm that acetylene is efficiently and selectively hydrogenated by such active $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{III}}{-}\mathrm{O}$ entities.
Overall, the presented strategy illustrates a very straightforward way to obtain, in multigram-scale, largely accessible and stabilized within MOFs channels catalytically active metal complexes and salts. In addition, the possibility to unveil the crystal structure of these host−guest materials goes beyond providing cutting-edge characterizations of such host−guest systems, which is of fundamental importance to select the most appropriate catalytic reactions and to unveil their mechanisms.
## STEP-BY-STEP CHEMICAL SYNTHESIS OF SUBNANOMETER METAL CLUSTERS AND SINGLE ATOMS WITHIN MOFS
The emergence of metal nanoparticles47 (MNPs) at the end of last century resulted in a paradigmatic shift in science. More recently, a series of works have unveiled exceptional properties, such as luminescence, biocompatibility, antitumor, and superlative catalytic activities, for small aggregations of metal atoms, so-called sub-nanometer metal clusters (SNMCs),48,49 and single metal atom catalysts (SACs).50 However, the characterization and the gram-scale preparation of stable and welldefined ligand-free SNMCs and/or SACs is still highly challenging. This, in part, is a consequence of their tendency to agglomerate into larger MNPs due to the lack of stabilizing/ blocking ligands, whose presence, in turn, greatly decreases their catalytic activity. In this context, a variety of selected MOFs with tailored properties such as high robustness, crystallinity, and appropriate porosity might be the perfect platforms, owing to the previously described host−guest chemistry, to synthesize, stabilize, and characterize SNMCs within their pores. Our synthetic strategy is divided in two main blocks: (i) The preparation of highly robust and crystalline MOFs with appropriate functional empty space and (ii) the application of PSMs for the assembly of such unique ultrasmall SMNCs or the reduction and stabilization of SACs, which are very difficult to be obtained and, especially, stabilized outside the channels.
The first example reported was the clean multigram-scale preparation of well-defined ligand-free $\mathrm{Pd}_{4}$ clusters within the c h a n n e l s o f t h e p r e v i o u s l y d e s c r i b e d a n i o n i c $\mathrm{Ni_{2}^{I I}\{N i_{4}^{I I}[C u_{\ 2}^{I I}(M e_{3}m p b a)_{2}]_{3}\}{\cdot}54H_{2}O}$ MOF.51 The process involved three consecutive PS steps: (i) A transmetalation and cation exchange processes involving the replacement of all magnesium(II) cations in an ancestor MOF of formula $\mathrm{Mg_{\2}^{\mathrm{\check{II}}}\big\{M g_{\ 4}^{\mathrm{II}}\big[C u_{\ 2}^{\mathrm{II}}\big(M e_{\mathrm{3}}m p b a\big)_{\mathrm{2}}\big]_{\mathrm{3}}\big\}\cdot54H_{\mathrm{2}}O}$ MOF to give the CuNi MOF (Figure 6a,b). (ii) The exchange of the nickel(II) cations within the pores by palladium $(\mathrm{II})$ ones (Figure 6c). (iii) The controlled insertion of the reducing agent $\left(\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}\right)$ to synthesize in situ the naked $\mathrm{Pd}_{4}\mathrm{NCs}$ (Figure 6d). Here, SCXRD played a 2-fold fundamental role, elucidating the crystal structure in every step and endowing us with key insights on two structural factors that seem to be at the origin of such controlled formation of SMNCs, i.e., number of inserted cations and their organization in the confined space. Figure 6c allows the observation of a very homogeneous distribution of $\mathrm{Pd}^{2+}$ cations along the channels, which are situated in specific positions interacting with the carboxylate-oxygen atoms from the network and limited in number by the anionic charge of the MOF. As a result, the palladium atoms have restrained movement capacity which favors the formation of SMNCs and prevents their agregation into larger MNPs. The $\mathrm{Pd}_{4}$ NCs are also stabilized by different weak supramolecular interactions (Figure 6e) which impose such uncommon linear structure and prevent their leaching during catalytic processes.
The resulting $[\mathbf{Pd}_{4}]^{2+}(\bar{\boldsymbol{a}}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{O}\mathbf{F}$ exhibits outstanding catalytic behavior in different carbene-insertion reactions, with TONs at least 1 order of magnitude higher than those of the state-ofthe-art catalysts. In addition, the MOF-supported $\mathrm{Pd}_{4}$ NCs exhibited great recyclability (at least 20 catalytic cycles without apparent loss of catalytic activity, Figure 6f) and they could be also used in-flow, simulating the conditions of a continuous reactor, thus opening the window for their potential real application.
The same strategy was used to stabilize $\mathrm{Pt_{1}}^{1+}\mathrm{SACs},^{52}$ within the same anionic MOF used for the growth of $\mathrm{Pd}_{4}$ NCs. In this case, following a two-step PS process, consisting first on the insertion of $\bar{\mathrm{Pt}}^{2+}$ cations within MOF channels and second their in situ reduction with $\mathrm{NaBH}_{4},$ the gram-scale synthesis of structurally and electronically well-defined $\mathrm{Pt}_{1}^{1+}$ SACs was achieved (Figure 7a). SC-XRD gave us precious insights of how water molecules surrounding $\mathrm{Pt}_{1}^{1+}$ atoms play a 2-fold role by stabilizing and isolating the $\mathrm{{Pt}(I)}$ SACs and also by regulating such an uncommon charge for the Pt atoms as well as the adsorption of reactants (vide infra). Indeed, all these points have important implications in the catalytic activity of the $\mathbf{Pt_{1}}^{1+}\mathbf{\mathcal{(a\mathbf{MOF}}}$ . This hybrid material is capable to catalyze, very efficiently, the low-temperature WGSR (water−gas shift reaction): $\mathrm{CO}+\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\rightarrow\mathrm{CO}_{2}+\mathrm{H}_{2}$ (Figure 7b). Remarkably, the combination of experimental results and theoretical calculations, based on the real crystal structure of the final $\mathbf{Pt}_{1}^{~1+}(\mathcal{\pmb{a}}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{O}\mathbf{F}_{.}$ , allowed one to unveil the plausible mechanism governing such a catalytic reaction. Thus, at least two water molecules surrounding the $\mathrm{Pt}_{1}^{\ 1+}$ SAC participate in the reaction attacking CO, and ultimately, both oxygen atoms in $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ come from these water molecules (Figure 7c).
A very similar strategy, was also carried out with the Lmethionine-derived MOF of formula $\{{\mathbf{Ca}}^{\mathrm{II}}{\mathbf{Cu}}^{\mathrm{II}}{}_{6}[(S,S)-$ me$\mathrm{\thox]}_{3}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})]$ (Figure 2b). In so doing, the gramscale synthesis and stabilization of $\mathrm{Pt}_{2}^{0}\mathrm{NCs},^{53}$ homogeneously distributed and densely packaged within the channels of the MOF, were achieved (Figure 8). The synthetic approach consisted of the application of a two-step PS process. First, the selected MOF was soaked within $\mathrm{K}_{2}[\mathrm{PtCl}_{4}]$ solutions. In agreement with the well-known affinity of sulfur atoms for platinum ions, it was observed a very efficient capture and homogeneous distribution of $\mathrm{Pt}^{2+}$ salts along the channels (Figure 8, left). The homogeneous distribution and limited number of metal atoms, by the number of sulfur containing groups, together with the confined space offered by these channels and the restrained movement of these metal salts, effectively anchored to the functionalities of the walls, allowed the formation of ligand-free ultrasmall SNMCs instead of larger MNPs. Indeed, after the introduction of the reducing agent $\left(\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}\right)$ in the second PS step, well-defined $\mathrm{Pt}_{2}^{\mathrm{~\tiny~\breve~{~0~}~}}$ clusters units were obtained in the final host−guest material $\mathbf{Pt}_{2}^{0}@\mathbf{MOF}$ (Figure 8, right). Again, the resolution of the crystal structures of these host−guest materials allowed one to propose a plausible formation mechanism for such tiny $\mathrm{Pt}_{2}^{0}$ NCs, where the flexibility of thio-alkyl groups play a key role approaching two neighboring $\mathrm{Pt}^{2+}{-\dot{S}}$ units to build the $\mathrm{Pt}_{2}^{0}$ ones.
The catalytic activity of $\mathbf{Pt}_{2}^{0}@\mathbf{MOF}$ was evaluated for three energetically-costly industrial gas-phase reactions such as hydrogen cyanide (HCN) production, alkene hydrogenations, and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ methanation. $\mathbf{Pt}_{2}^{\mathsf{\widehat{0}}}(\varpi\mathbf{MOF}$ was capable to efficiently catalyze the three processes at low-temperatures $(25-140\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ , confirming the great capacity of these SNMCs to activate such refractory molecules. For instance, $\mathbf{Pt}_{2}^{0}@\mathbf{MOF}$ exhibited outstanding catalytic activity for the room temperature formation of $\mathrm{NH_{4}C N}$ from $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ and CO, revealing the largest catalytic activity among the wide variety of Pt catalysts tested (including Pt salts, complexes, and supported $\mathrm{NPs}\mathrm{\i}$ ). On the other hand, $\mathbf{Pt}_{2}^{0}(\bar{\varrho}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{O}\bar{\mathbf{F}}$ also catalyzes very efficiently the Sabatier reaction $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ methanation, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}+4\mathrm{H}_{2}\Leftrightarrow\mathrm{CH}_{4}+$ $2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\dot{]}$ ) at temperatures lower than $150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . This remarkable high activity of $\mathrm{Pt}_{2}^{0}$ in the low-temperature region agrees with their low calculated energy of activation $\left(E_{\mathrm{a}}\ {=}\ 35\ \bar{\mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}}}\right)$ , which is about one-half of that shown by commercial catalysts $(E_{\mathrm{a}}\approx65\mathrm{\kJ\mol^{-1}},$ ). Finally, $\mathbf{Pt}_{2}^{0}(\bar{\varrho}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{O}\mathbf{\dot{F}}$ was also capable to catalyze, efficiently, the ethylene hydrogenation in flow with a low calculated activation energy $\left(E_{\mathrm{a}}\ \tilde{\approx}\ 32\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}}\right)$ , which allows a reaction temperature of $60\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ below that used by catalysts such as MNPs $(200-400\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ .
These results validate the proposed strategy for the MOFdriven gram-scale preparation and stabilization of well-defined ligand-free SNMCs and/or SACs with outstanding catalytic activity for different reactions of interest. The synthetic approach relies on the use of highly crystalline and robust
MOFs that allow both the use of PSMs and SC-XRD. A fine control of the porous empty space is mandatory to control the growth of SNMCs and/or SACs, since it regulates the number of metal atoms inserted and its homogeneous distribution within MOFs channels.
## MOF-DRIVEN SELF-ASSEMBLY OF CATALYTICALLY ACTIVE SUPRAMOLECULAR ASSEMBLIES
Supramolecular chemistry54 has offered many examples of supramolecular coordination complexes (SCCs), which exhibit fascinating architectures and thrilling functionalities. However, the application of SCCs in catalysis has been somehow hampered because of a lack of spatial three-dimensional organization and limited structural robustness under reaction conditions. In addition, the self-assembly of SCCs have been always limited to homogeneous chemistry in solution. This, inherently, has led to the construction of supramolecular architectures where the pivotal metal ions of the assembly are fully coordinated, which have mitigated its catalytic application.
In such a context, our group faced the 2-fold challenge of obtaining more structurally robust and spatially well-organized SCCs and exploiting the potential of the pivotal metal atoms building the SCCs. In doing so, we have taken advantage of the microenvironment of oxamato- and oxamidato-based MOFs channels and its exceptional features for the stepwise fabrication and characterization of complex functional SCCs, otherwise hardly accessible, within their large pores and showing enhanced robustness and catalytic properties.
The synthetic strategy, similar to that used for the synthesis of SNMCs and SACs, relies on the use of selected crystalline and robust MOFs as chemical reactors for the in situ selfassembly of these SCCs. Once appropriate MOFs are prepared/selected, a two-step PS methodology is used to construct these supramolecular assemblies. The first step consists of the controlled insertion of the metal cations (Figure 9). In this point, the resolution of the crystal structure of this intermediate phase becomes crucial, as it allows to observe metal atom positions and distances. On the basis of these parameters, appropriate ligands can be selected and inserted in the second PS step to self-assemble SCCs within MOFs channels (Figure 9).
Applying this approach, we recently reported the preparation of two mechanically bonded in situ self-assembled catalytically active ${\tt S C C s}(\varpi\mathrm{MOFs}$ .55 Starting from the anionic $\mathrm{Ni^{II}}_{2}\{\mathrm{Ni^{II}}_{4}[\mathrm{Cu^{II}}_{2}(\mathrm{Me}_{3}\mathrm{mpba)}_{2}]_{3}\}\cdot54\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ MOF (Figure 2a), the first PS step consisted of replacing $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ cations hosted in the pores (Figure 2a) by $\mathrm{Pd}^{2+}$ ones (Figure 10). The crystal structure of this intermediate phase allowed the direct visualization of the position and distances between $\mathrm{Pd}^{2+}$ units, which situate in the larger octagonal pores of the MOF forming dinuclear cationic entities, the number of Pd atoms, and the available empty space. After a careful analysis, two aromatic dipyridine ligands $\mathrm{{\dot{L}}_{1}}$ and $\mathbf{L}_{2}$ where $\mathrm{L}_{1}=1,2\cdot$ di(pyridn-4-yl)ethyne and $\begin{array}{l l}{{\mathrm{L}_{2}}}&{{=}}\end{array}$ methyl 3,5-bis(pyridine-4- ylethynyl)benzoate) were selected, and the $\mathbf{Pd}^{2+}\varpi\mathbf{MOF}$ was soaked in $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CN}$ solutions of $\mathrm{L}_{1}$ and $\mathrm{L}_{2}$ . As a result, two novel unique palladium supramolecular assemblies were found within the channels of the MOF. In particular, a new $\mathrm{Pd}_{~8}^{\mathrm{II}}$ $(\mathbf{Pd}_{8}^{\mathrm{II}}(\pmb{\mathrm{p}}_{\mathrm{~}}\mathbf{d}^{\mathrm{~}})$ MOF) square metal−organic polygon when using the linear ligand $\mathbf{L}_{1}$ (Figure 10) whereas a $\mathrm{Pd}_{16}^{\mathrm{II}}$ supramolecular assembly $\mathbf{(Pd^{II}}_{16}^{-}@\mathbf{MOF})$ with the tripodal bended ligand $\mathbf{L}_{2}$ (Figure 10) were observed. Remarkably, the crystal structure of both supramolecular constructs could be ascertained with atomic precision, for the first time in a MOF as far as we know, by SC-XRD (Figure 10). The $\mathrm{Pd}_{8}^{\mathrm{II}}$ metal−organic polygons, assembled by covalent bonds, exhibit a square structure where the dipalladium(II) units situate at the corners of each square, whereas the $\mathrm{Pd}_{16}^{\mathrm{II}}$ supramolecular assemblies combine covalent bonds and strong H-bonds involving also water molecules. In addition, both supramolecular assemblies are further stabilized by mechanical-bonds with the walls of the MOF channels (Figure 11), which undoubtedly contribute to enhance the robustness of such assembled metal complexes and, thus, their stability under catalytic reaction conditions.
Aiming at confirming the proposed hypothesis, the catalytic performance in metal-based reactions of both materials was studied. In particular, for the coupling of different boronic acids and alkynes, they showed improved catalytic activity in comparison with $\mathbf{Pd}^{2+}(\mathcal{O}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{O}\mathbf{F}$ and other $\mathrm{Pd^{II}}$ complexes and traditional metal−organic cages, which tend to decompose, and a good recyclability. In addition, the confined limited space within the $\mathrm{\dot{P}d_{\mathrm{~8~}}^{I I}}$ and $\mathrm{Pd}_{16}^{\mathrm{II}}$ compounds leads to enhanced selectivity in comparison with the other tested $\mathrm{Pd}^{\mathrm{II}}$ species. Finally, a third heterometallic PdAu supramolecular construct was also assembled within this MOF and reported in this work as a preliminary result. This compound exhibits interesting catalytic properties, but further studies are still necessary to fully characterize its structure.
## CONCLUSIONS
In this Account, we have described our latest advances on the use of the unique host−guest chemistry and high robustness and crystallinity of oxamato- and oxamidato-based MOFs to start unveiling the great potential of MOFs as chemical nanoreactors. The particular examples shown above are simple examples of the singular and unprecedented opportunities that offer the empty space of MOFs. Thus, having in mind the vast number of reported MOFs, it is tempting to think that a new dimension in the field is waiting to be uncovered. However, this would not be an easy task and several challenges have to be overcome. In this sense, it would be necessary to make stronger efforts to develop materials with both the needed crystallinity and structural robustness, to resist the PSMs and to take advantage of SC-XRD, and try to achieve materials with enhanced thermal stability, as close as possible to other microporous materials with embedded metal species.27−29 MOFs with remarkable stability in different media have been thoroughly developed, but most frequently it has not been coupled with appealing crystals. The strengthening of this point will boost not only this emerging application of MOFs as chemical nanoreactors but also all of the entire field through cross-fertilization.
MOFs field have been started to install in a fierce competition to develop the most performant material in different applications. In particular, the present strategy has led to the preparation of unique catalytically active MOF-driven metal species that, in some cases, may compete with the stateof-the-art industrial catalysts. Moreover, besides the positive advances that this has enabled, we consider that basic research and deeper comprehension of the singular possibilities of MOFs channels should be more intensively evaluated. This, in a first stage could be implemented with the validation of the design principles shown above to MOFs with other organic and inorganic constitutents, and then, to MOFs with a higher degree of complexity and heterogenity within their channels, such as multivariate-MOFs (MTV-MOFs).56 This would help us to gain precious insight on the key parameters that rule the synthesis and self-assembly of functional species within MOFs channels. We believe that this is an appealing way to start mastering this type of chemistry in confined space as it has been done with covalent chemistry of small molecules in a homogeneous phase.
